": 6.09, "analyst_adjustment": -0.59, "key_factors": ["Prediction markets price Mendoza around 82-91% to win", "Most expert raters call the seat a flip, though Cook, Inside Elections and Sabato still say Tossup", "Mendoza has outraised Ciscomani with more cash on hand, but the incumbent retains a slight cash edge and low outside spending", "No recent high-quality public polls; the race rests on fundamentals and ratings"], "rationale": "Markets and most raters point to a Democratic flip in this even-PVI open-seat-against-incumbent contest, and Mendoza's fundraising edge supports that direction. With no usable recent polls and a sitting incumbent defending the seat, the forecast stays at a modest Democratic edge rather than a blowout.", "watch": "Any late independent polling or a strong debate/fundraising stretch for Ciscomani could pull this back toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Juan Ciscomani (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's 6th District is an even partisan split, and first-term Republican Juan Ciscomani is seeking reelection against Democrat JoAnna Mendoza, a military-intelligence veteran. Mendoza has raised about $7.4 million to Ciscomani's $6.2 million, though the incumbent holds a slight cash-on-hand advantage and outside spending has been minimal so far. The two have met in several debates focused on the economy and inflation.\n\nForecasters split between Tossup and Lean Democratic, with several calling the seat a flip, and prediction markets price Mendoza's chances in the low-to-mid 80s. With little recent public polling, the race leans Democratic on fundamentals, ratings, and money, but remains genuinely competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8163, "margin": 5.47}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8157, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8181, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8226